entry yield
term: Nuclear Yield
difficulty: advanced
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Yield.webp
related_terms: chain-reaction
related_terms: critical-mass
related_terms: edward-teller
related_terms: nuclear-fusion
quick_facts: Measured in TNT equivalents: kt, Mt
quick_facts: Hiroshima: ~15 kt · largest ever tested: 50 Mt (Tsar Bomba)
quick_facts: Fission primary ignites the fusion secondary in modern weapons
quick_facts: Airbursts maximize blast; ground bursts maximize fallout
quick_facts: 1 kt = 4.184 × 10¹² joules

sources:
    {"organization":"CTBTO","title":"Nuclear Testing","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/"}

definition:
    The explosive energy of a nuclear weapon, quoted in tons or megatons of TNT equivalent (1 kiloton = 4.184 trillion joules). Hiroshima's Little Boy delivered about 15 kilotons from a single fission stage; modern thermonuclear warheads reach hundreds of kilotons to megatons by wrapping a fission trigger around fusion fuel. Yield splits between blast, thermal pulse, prompt radiation, and fallout — whose severity depends on burst height and design choices.
